'''timber<sup>3</sup>''' (tim′bėr), ''n.'' [Also ''timbre;'' < ME. *''timbre, tymbre,'' < OF. ''timbre'', a helmet, crest, timber, F. also stamp, = Pr. ''timbre'' = Sp. ''timbre'' = Pg. ''timbre'', a crest, helmet; prob. so called as being shaped like a kettledrum, < L. ''tympanum'', a drum: see ''tympan, tympanum''. For the change, ''timbre'' < ''tympanum'', cf. ''ordre'' < ''ordinem'' (see ''order''). Cf. ''timbre''<sup>2</sup>, ''timbre''<sup>3</sup>, from the same source.] In ''her.'', originally, the crest; hence, in modern heraldry, the helmet, miter, coronet, etc., when placed over the arms in a complete achievement.

